# Worked solutions: 3.44 - 3.46 (stability) and 4.1 - 4.37 (root locus)
S = {
"3.44": ("D", "Routh criterion with a zero in the loop gain.", "$s(s^2+5s+9)+K(1-s)=s^3+5s^2+(9-K)s+K=0$.\n$s^1$ element: $\\dfrac{5(9-K)-K}{5}>0\\Rightarrow K<7.5$, and $K>0$.\n\nMaximum $K=7.5$: **(d)**."),
"3.45": ("D", "Routh array: sign changes in the first column count the RHP roots.", "| $s^6$ | 5 | 12 | 100 | 200 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| $s^5$ | 8 | 20 | 150 | |\n| $s^4$ | $-0.5$ | 6.25 | 200 | |\n| $s^3$ | 120 | 3350 | | |\n| $s^2$ | 20.2 | 200 | | |\n| $s^1$ | 2162 | | | |\n| $s^0$ | 200 | | | |\n\nFirst column: $5,\\ 8,\\ -0.5,\\ 120,\\ 20.2,\\ 2162,\\ 200$. There are two sign changes, so two poles are in the right half-plane and the system is unstable: **(d)**."),
"3.46": ("C", "Routh criterion on $s^3+3s^2+2s+K$.", "The $s^1$ element is $\\dfrac{3\\cdot2-K}{3}>0\\Rightarrow K<6$, and $K>0$.\n\n$0<K<6$: **(c)**."),
"4.1": ("D", "Centroid $=\\dfrac{\\sum\\text{poles}-\\sum\\text{zeros}}{P-Z}$.", "Poles: $0,-1,-3$; no zeros.\n$\\sigma_A=\\dfrac{0-1-3}{3}=-1.33$: **(d)**."),
"4.2": ("B", "Breakaway points: $dK/ds=0$.", "$K=-s(s+4)(s^2+4s+5)=-u(u+5)$ with $u=s^2+4s$.\n$\\dfrac{dK}{ds}=-(2u+5)(2s+4)=0\\Rightarrow s=-2$ or $s^2+4s+2.5=0\\Rightarrow s=-2\\pm1.22$.\nThe three points $-0.78,\\ -2,\\ -3.22$ are all real, between the poles at $0$ and $-4$, and $K>0$ at each: three real breakaway points: **(b)**."),
"4.3": ("C", "Zeros at infinity $=P-Z$.", "$P=4$ (at $0,-2,-1\\pm j$) and $Z=1$, so $P-Z=3$ zeros at infinity: **(c)**."),
"4.4": ("D", "Branch starts and asymptotes identify the poles.", "Three branches start from two points: a double pole at the origin and one real pole at $-T_1$ on the negative real axis. The asymptotes are at $\\pm60^\\circ$ and $180^\\circ$, which is right for $P-Z=3$.\n\n$G(s)=\\dfrac{K}{s^2(sT_1+1)}$: **(d)**."),
"4.5": ("B", "Instrument for sketching root locus.", "The *Spirule* is the mechanical device used to measure angles and plot root loci: **(b)**."),
"4.6": ("C", "Real-axis rule: odd number of poles + zeros to the right.", "Poles at $0,-3,-4$; zero at $-1$.\n- $-1<s<0$: 1 to the right, odd: on the locus.\n- $-3<s<-1$: 2 to the right, even: not on it.\n- $-4<s<-3$: 3 to the right, odd: on the locus.\n- $s<-4$: 4 to the right, even: not on it.\n\nOf the options, between $-3$ and $-4$: **(c)**."),
"4.7": ("D", "Breakaway circle centred on the zero.", "The loci leave the real axis between the poles at $0$ and $-1$, move round a circle of radius $\\sqrt2$ centred at $-2$, and return to the real axis left of $-2$. A circle centred on a zero occurs for two poles and one zero, with radius $\\sqrt{1\\cdot2}=\\sqrt2$.\n\nPoles $0,-1$, zero $-2$: $G=\\dfrac{K(s+2)}{s(s+1)}$: **(d)**."),
"4.8": ("B", "Software for accurate root locus.", "MATLAB (`rlocus`) draws an accurate root locus: **(b)**."),
"4.9": ("B", "Imaginary-axis crossing from Routh.", "Setting the $s^1$ row of the Routh array to zero gives the gain at which the locus crosses the $j\\omega$ axis, and the auxiliary equation gives the crossing frequency: **(b)**."),
"4.10": ("B", "Asymptote angles $\\theta=\\dfrac{(2k+1)180^\\circ}{P-Z}$.", "The asymptotes make angles $\\dfrac{(2k+1)\\pi}{p-z}$ with the real axis: **(b)**."),
"4.11": ("B", "Branches to infinity and the real-axis rule.", "(A) $P-Z$ branches go to infinity: true.\n(R) Real-axis segments are on the locus where the count of poles and zeros to their right is odd: true.\nThe two statements are unrelated, so (R) does not explain (A): **(b)**."),
"4.12": ("A", "Symmetry of the root locus.", "The root locus is symmetrical about the *real* axis, not the $j\\omega$ axis. The other three statements are standard properties. So the false one is (a): **(a)**."),
"4.13": ("B", "Number of branches.", "Each branch starts at an open-loop pole, so there are as many separate loci as poles of $G(s)H(s)$ (assuming $P\\ge Z$): **(b)**."),
"4.14": ("C", "Centroid.", "Poles: $0,-1,-2,-5$ (sum $-8$); zero: $-3$.\n$\\sigma_A=\\dfrac{-8-(-3)}{4-1}=-\\dfrac53$: **(c)**."),
"4.15": ("C", "Asymptotes at $\\pm90^\\circ$ through the centroid.", "$P-Z=2$ gives asymptotes at $\\pm90^\\circ$. For $\\dfrac K{(s+2)^2}$ the two branches leave the double pole at $-2$ and run along the vertical line $\\sigma=-2$, parallel to the imaginary axis: **(c)**."),
"4.16": ("C", "Branches going to infinity.", "Each zero absorbs one branch; the remaining $P-Z$ go to infinity: **(c)**."),
"4.17": ("B", "Check each plot against the construction rules.", "For two poles and one zero the locus leaves the axis on a circle *centred on the zero*.\n- Plot 1: complex poles and a zero to their left; the loop closes through the zero: valid.\n- Plot 2: poles at $-0.2,-1.05$ and zero at $-1.63$; the circle should be centred on the zero ($\\approx-1.63$, radius $\\approx0.91$). The drawn circle is centred at $-1.2$: **wrong**.\n- Plot 3 and 4: pole, zero, and asymptote directions are consistent.\n\nThe incorrect plot is 2: **(b)**."),
"4.18": ("B", "Basic properties.", "The root locus is symmetrical about the real axis because complex roots occur in conjugate pairs. Real-axis segments lie where an *odd* number of poles and zeros are to the right, so (c) and (d) are wrong: **(b)**."),
"4.19": ("C", "Definition of root locus.", "The root locus is the path of the characteristic-equation roots as a system parameter (usually the gain $K$) varies: **(c)**."),
"4.20": ("A", "Breakaway/break-in: $dK/ds=0$.", "$K=-\\dfrac{s^2+2s+2}{s+2}$.\n$\\dfrac{dK}{ds}=0\\Rightarrow(2s+2)(s+2)-(s^2+2s+2)=s^2+4s+2=0\\Rightarrow s=-2\\pm\\sqrt2$.\nOnly $-3.414$ lies on the real-axis locus (left of the zero at $-2$): **(a)**."),
"4.21": ("D", "Number of branches $=\\max(P,Z)$.", "The equation is $1+\\dfrac{K(s+1)}{s(s+2)(s+3)}=0$ with $P=3$ and $Z=1$. The number of branches is $\\max(P,Z)=3$: **(d)**."),
"4.22": ("D", "$K\\to0$ gives the poles.", "At $K=0$ the characteristic equation reduces to $D(s)=0$, so the roots coincide with the open-loop poles: **(d)**."),
"4.23": ("A", "Effect of adding a zero.", "A zero attracts the locus towards itself and bends it to the left, which increases relative stability: **(a)**."),
"4.24": ("B", "Locus at $K=\\pm\\infty$.", "As $|K|\\to\\infty$ the roots tend to the open-loop zeros (and to infinity): **(b)**."),
"4.25": ("C", "Centroid.", "Poles: $0,-4,-1\\pm j$ (sum $-6$); zero: $-1$.\n$\\sigma_A=\\dfrac{-6-(-1)}{4-1}=-\\dfrac53$: **(c)**."),
"4.26": ("B", "Locus at $K=0$.", "At $K=0$ the roots are the open-loop poles: **(b)**."),
"4.27": ("B", "Damping ratio 0.707 means poles at $45^\\circ$.", "$s^2+4s+3+4K=0\\Rightarrow s=-2\\pm\\sqrt{1-4K}$.\nFor $\\xi=0.707$ the poles are at $-2\\pm j2$, so $1-4K=-4\\Rightarrow K=\\dfrac54$: **(b)**."),
"4.28": ("D", "Number of branches $=\\max(P,Z)$.", "Each branch begins at an open-loop pole and ends at a zero (or infinity), so the number of loci is the larger of the number of poles and zeros: **(d)**."),
"4.29": ("B", "Symmetry of the locus.", "(I) True, because complex roots come in conjugate pairs. (II) is also true when the pole-zero pattern is itself symmetrical, but it is not the reason behind (I).\n\nBoth true, (II) not the explanation: **(b)**."),
"4.30": ("A", "Centroid of the asymptotes.", "Poles: $0,-2,-1\\pm j2$; no zeros. Sum of poles $=-4$.\n$\\sigma_A=\\dfrac{-4}{4}=-1$, on the real axis, so the coordinates are $(-1,\\ 0)$: **(a)**."),
"4.31": ("D", "No imaginary-axis crossing means no finite critical gain.", "If the locus never crosses the $j\\omega$ axis, the gain can be increased indefinitely without instability, so the gain margin is infinite: **(d)**."),
"4.32": ("C", "Locus passes through $s=0$ when the CE is satisfied there.", "$s^3+4s^2+s-6+K=0$ at $s=0$: $-6+K=0\\Rightarrow K=6$: **(c)**."),
"4.33": ("C", "Oscillatory (marginal) gain from Routh.", "$s^3+6s^2+10s+K=0$. The $s^1$ element is $\\dfrac{60-K}{6}=0\\Rightarrow K=60$.\nAuxiliary equation $6s^2+60=0\\Rightarrow s=\\pm j\\sqrt{10}$, so $\\omega=3.16$ rad/s.\n\n**(c)**."),
"4.34": ("C", "Branches to infinity $=P-Z$.", "When $P=Z$, every branch ends on a finite zero, so none tends to infinity: **(c)**."),
"4.35": ("C", "Closed-loop poles: $1+G=0$.", "$(s+2)^2+1=0\\Rightarrow s=-2\\pm j$: **(c)**."),
"4.36": ("B", "Number of branches.", "$P=3$ (poles at $0,-4,-5$) and $Z=1$, so there are $\\max(P,Z)=3$ branches: **(b)**."),
"4.37": ("B", "Breakaway points from $dK/ds=0$.", "$K=-\\dfrac{s^2+2s+3}{s+2}$.\n$\\dfrac{dK}{ds}=0\\Rightarrow(2s+2)(s+2)-(s^2+2s+3)=s^2+4s+1=0\\Rightarrow s=-2\\pm\\sqrt3$, i.e. $-0.268$ or $-3.732$.\nThe real-axis locus lies left of the zero at $-2$, so only $-3.732$ is on it. It is a break-in point: **(b)**."),
}
